WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.004
Imagine this.

00:00:01.224 --> 00:00:03.819
You're in Spain, with one message for
Norway.

00:00:04.059 --> 00:00:05.433
Do you call now, or wait?

00:00:05.708 --> 00:00:07.570
That choice turned out to matter.

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:10.908
We tried five ways of routing the same
radio traffic.

00:00:10.908 --> 00:00:13.334
Watch what happens to this one message.

00:00:13.625 --> 00:00:14.828
Here’s how we set it up.

00:00:14.969 --> 00:00:18.965
We varied the size of the network, the
traffic, and the faults.

00:00:18.965 --> 00:00:23.500
Twenty matched seeds per case gave us
twenty-seven hundred policy runs.

00:00:23.792 --> 00:00:28.312
Every router got the same listening
windows and the same underlying radio

00:00:28.312 --> 00:00:29.192
conditions.

00:00:29.382 --> 00:00:32.330
Who actually called? That was up to each
router.

00:00:32.583 --> 00:00:35.333
Start in Spain. This is message six.

00:00:35.493 --> 00:00:37.703
At fifteen oh six, universal time,

00:00:37.703 --> 00:00:41.276
the earliest-arrival router calls a relay
in Britain.

00:00:41.542 --> 00:00:42.899
Britain gets the message.

00:00:43.119 --> 00:00:47.142
Spain never gets the acknowledgment. So
the relay has a copy,

00:00:47.142 --> 00:00:50.039
and the sender has no idea the handoff
worked.

00:00:50.292 --> 00:00:51.870
Let's go back to that minute.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:56.492
This router combines the propagation
forecast with its own call history.

00:00:56.492 --> 00:00:59.034
It lets the British window go by.

00:00:59.292 --> 00:01:01.021
Why this particular message?

00:01:01.241 --> 00:01:02.212
In seed seventeen,

00:01:02.212 --> 00:01:06.872
it's the first one where the two
strategies ended up with different
delivery

00:01:06.872 --> 00:01:07.489
outcomes.

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:10.251
Ten minutes later, Spain calls France.

00:01:10.381 --> 00:01:14.770
The colors on the globe are from the
ionospheric grid at fifteen fifteen,

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:16.419
one minute before the call.

00:01:16.708 --> 00:01:23.348
Look halfway along the path. The F2 peak
is 267 kilometers up,

00:01:23.348 --> 00:01:28.063
and vertical electron content is 25.4 TEC
units.

00:01:28.243 --> 00:01:31.816
Those are modeled conditions at the time
of a simulated call.

00:01:32.083 --> 00:01:33.908
Now watch the copy in Britain.

00:01:34.068 --> 00:01:39.115
At fifteen forty, the relay sends it to
Norway. One minute later, it's there.

00:01:39.375 --> 00:01:43.324
Back in the combined-policy run, the
message is still waiting.

00:01:43.524 --> 00:01:46.566
Fifteen fifty-five. Its deadline passes.

00:01:46.833 --> 00:01:48.856
Here are the actual terminal records.

00:01:49.006 --> 00:01:53.493
On the left, read down the tmux pane.
You'll see collisions,

00:01:53.493 --> 00:01:56.946
a lost acknowledgment, and finally the
transfer to Norway.

00:01:57.208 --> 00:01:59.120
On the right, the last call fails.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:02.486
Same message, same seed, same starting
conditions.

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:04.154
But that's one message.

00:02:04.354 --> 00:02:05.716
What happened to the rest?

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:11.260
In light traffic, forecast plus history
delivered 72.5 percent,

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:12.927
weighted by priority.

00:02:13.087 --> 00:02:20.218
At commissioned load it fell to 26.8
percent. Earliest arrival reached 32.1.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:22.330
Could all six messages have fit?

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:26.760
Yes. An offline search found a conflict-
free schedule that delivered every

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:27.376
one.

00:02:27.667 --> 00:02:29.894
The best online router got one through.

00:02:30.094 --> 00:02:32.587
The offline search had one huge advantage.

00:02:32.587 --> 00:02:35.231
It knew which calls were going to succeed.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:38.240
Zoom out to a thousand simulated stations.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.360
With open listening windows at
commissioned load,

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.627
every strategy came back with zero
deliveries.

00:02:44.917 --> 00:02:47.779
So we tried assigning one caller to each
window.

00:02:47.949 --> 00:02:51.997
The globe is following a message that made
it through under those

00:02:51.997 --> 00:02:52.450
reservations.

00:02:52.708 --> 00:02:57.699
Across twenty seeds, earliest arrival went
from zero deliveries to seven.

00:02:57.959 --> 00:03:00.332
Seven, out of twenty thousand offers.

00:03:00.583 --> 00:03:01.743
In four hours,

00:03:01.743 --> 00:03:05.823
only twenty-four of the nine hundred and
ninety-four access stations got a

00:03:05.823 --> 00:03:06.354
turn.

00:03:06.534 --> 00:03:08.404
Most never had a chance to send.

00:03:08.667 --> 00:03:12.153
We tested the modems separately, over a
virtual cable.

00:03:12.293 --> 00:03:17.307
Five clean trials, and five with just
noise: every payload was confirmed.

00:03:17.307 --> 00:03:22.318
An echo or a severe return-path fade
stopped setup in every trial.

00:03:22.583 --> 00:03:24.245
Then we tried two fading paths.

00:03:24.405 --> 00:03:28.603
One of five quiet-channel runs connected;
none confirmed the message.

00:03:28.603 --> 00:03:31.361
In the disturbed channel, none connected.

00:03:31.625 --> 00:03:35.560
We also replayed over a thousand seconds
of recorded HF audio.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:39.045
Two decoders agreed on eighteen valid
receptions,

00:03:39.045 --> 00:03:43.698
with sixteen distinct frame hashes. The
transmitter logs were missing.

00:03:43.958 --> 00:03:46.821
Next time, I'd record both ends of the
same call.

00:03:47.041 --> 00:03:48.366
That's the missing piece.
